00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:14.350
RAM BHAVARAJU: So
since we started

00:06:14.350 --> 00:06:17.290
with this ride statistics
and the entire raw data,

00:06:17.290 --> 00:06:20.240
the sheer volume of data, I'll
take those two as an example

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:22.900
and then probably walk you
through the architecture.

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:25.450
Leaving what happens
inside the vehicle,

00:06:25.450 --> 00:06:29.530
let's see the data is now
pushed to an MQTT broker, what

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:32.770
we call as an IoT broker,
internet of things broker,

00:06:32.770 --> 00:06:35.950
all the way to start publishing
data into this broker.

00:06:35.950 --> 00:06:40.990
And this broker then publishes
further up into Kafka cluster.

00:06:40.990 --> 00:06:43.990
And this Artemis
broker's main role

00:06:43.990 --> 00:06:48.460
is just to send data
from the vehicles all

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:50.020
the way into a
Kafka cluster, where

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:52.960
the majority of the processing
of this huge volume of data

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:53.860
is happening.

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:56.680
And all of these workloads
are currently running on GKE.

00:06:56.680 --> 00:06:58.540
Once the data comes
into Kafka, we

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:01.990
have both the worlds, that is
the streaming part of Kafka.

00:07:01.990 --> 00:07:05.740
We also use the consumer's
or probably the publisher's

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:06.730
part of the Kafka.

00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:10.180
But when we looked at the volume
of data, what is coming in,

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:11.710
right, when I said
about gigabytes,

00:07:11.710 --> 00:07:14.350
terabytes, and mostly the
petabytes for cloud storage.

00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:18.620
So 70%+ of our use cases
are the raw data itself.

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:20.290
So this is where it
kind of gives you

00:07:20.290 --> 00:07:22.300
insights like product
metrics, gives you

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:24.100
insight into diagnostics
of the vehicle,

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:26.710
or probably look at
service and support,

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:29.320
and also a lot into the
research and development

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:31.040
of the vehicle itself.

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:33.490
And because it's so
important for every

00:07:33.490 --> 00:07:36.370
of these units to come together,
the raw data processing

00:07:36.370 --> 00:07:39.730
is also the quintessential
for us, right?

00:07:39.730 --> 00:07:43.120
So that is where we kind of
use these node pools, which

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:45.820
are within the K clusters.

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:47.980
And we use lots of
consumers in that case.

00:07:47.980 --> 00:07:51.010
All this data gets processed,
to a certain extent,

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:54.040
and then will get pushed
into Google Cloud Storage.

00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:56.650
We use Data Block and
Dataflow to a large extent.

00:07:56.650 --> 00:07:58.270
To query this
data, I was talking

00:07:58.270 --> 00:08:02.140
about all this internal
use cases of diagnostics

00:08:02.140 --> 00:08:04.600
or internal use
cases of R&amp;D research

00:08:04.600 --> 00:08:05.860
and development of things.

00:08:05.860 --> 00:08:08.650
For that, we use Data
Block and Dataflow.

00:08:08.650 --> 00:08:13.720
And we kind of use both these
tools to process the data

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:16.240
and also query the
data to a large extent.

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:18.940
Now, this is the
raw data part of it.

00:08:18.940 --> 00:08:21.770
Now, let's look at something
like a ride statistic,

00:08:21.770 --> 00:08:25.000
which is about 20%-30%
of the use cases.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:29.350
This 20%-30% of use cases
are more, what do you say,

00:08:29.350 --> 00:08:32.320
are more realistic
in this sense,

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:35.799
because you have to
do real time analysis.

00:08:35.799 --> 00:08:38.559
We have to do the real time
processing of this data,

00:08:38.559 --> 00:08:41.230
and then push it further
into an upstream,

00:08:41.230 --> 00:08:42.760
something like a
real-time database

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:45.340
called Firebase,
where all of our apps

00:08:45.340 --> 00:08:47.260
are like reading this data from.

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:50.740
So we have Ather app, Ather
app is like an ownership app,

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:54.430
which the customers would
be able to see the vehicle

00:08:54.430 --> 00:08:57.110
information on their app.

00:08:57.110 --> 00:08:59.290
This vehicle analytics,
again, requires

00:08:59.290 --> 00:09:02.020
lots of streaming of data,
so all these messages

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:04.120
which are coming in, by
the hundreds of thousands,

00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:08.650
and runs into billions over
a period of day or two.

00:09:08.650 --> 00:09:12.140
All this data gets
processed in near real time

00:09:12.140 --> 00:09:15.910
and then gets pushed into
Firebase, which then pushes

00:09:15.910 --> 00:09:17.793
that data into the Ather app.

00:09:17.793 --> 00:09:19.210
And that's where
the customers are

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:20.980
able to see the ride statistics.

00:09:20.980 --> 00:09:23.740
Again, we also use some
amount of batch jobs there.

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:25.660
And that is where we
use data proper Sparks,

00:09:25.660 --> 00:09:27.790
streaming what we
produce, and stream

00:09:27.790 --> 00:09:29.470
that data using those batches.

