00:10:02.663 --> 00:10:04.330
RAM BHAVARAJU: Issues
are common, right,

00:10:04.330 --> 00:10:08.530
and that's where the software
teams would learn, right?

00:10:08.530 --> 00:10:12.340
So we ran into this issue of
exhausting the node pools.

00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:15.670
So GKE runs on a concept
of a cluster, which

00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:21.820
is made of multiple different
node pools, odd parts what

00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:24.010
we call them, a part is
like a single unit which

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:26.380
runs any application per se.

00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:28.180
In this case, let's
take an example

00:10:28.180 --> 00:10:30.190
of a Kafka consumer,
which is nothing

00:10:30.190 --> 00:10:33.850
but an application, which
consumes the data from Kafka

00:10:33.850 --> 00:10:35.950
and does some action.

00:10:35.950 --> 00:10:39.700
And we ran out of
node pools on GKE,

00:10:39.700 --> 00:10:43.485
because GKE is a very good
hybrid of open source,

00:10:43.485 --> 00:10:47.400
like using your Kubernetes,
but also has managed services.

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:51.120
The entire control
plane is managed by GCP,

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:54.240
so the Google Cloud platform
itself in this case, right?

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:57.330
The node pools obviously have
this concept of auto-scaling.

00:10:57.330 --> 00:11:01.530
So we ran into a place where
one of our major consumers,

00:11:01.530 --> 00:11:04.560
and it has a big
work pool load that's

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:07.920
about almost 180 node
pools in that case,

00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:11.700
was trying to auto-scale
itself, and it ran out of nodes.

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:15.000
And what happened was that
no one knew, once you run out

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:18.180
of the resources, that is you
exhausted the entire node pool,

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:21.060
you have to wait until
you get any backup.

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:23.160
In this case, what
happened is that we

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:26.370
are in that region within
India where we did not

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:28.560
do multi-zone clusters
at that point in time,

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:31.040
because we wanted a
majority of our workplace

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:34.380
was running on a single
zone at that case rate.

00:11:34.380 --> 00:11:36.817
The entire node pool went into
something called a security

00:11:36.817 --> 00:11:39.150
mode, because the control
frame thinks that, OK, someone

00:11:39.150 --> 00:11:43.830
is trying to scale up way
beyond the node's actual limits.

00:11:43.830 --> 00:11:47.310
And now you don't know what to
do with it, because you're not

00:11:47.310 --> 00:11:48.610
able to do that.

00:11:48.610 --> 00:11:51.160
Then the cluster finds
out, saying that, OK,

00:11:51.160 --> 00:11:52.590
I don't have enough resources.

00:11:52.590 --> 00:11:56.850
Now I have to fix it, because
I have to allocate the existing

00:11:56.850 --> 00:11:57.870
resources itself.

00:11:57.870 --> 00:11:59.400
Something like that happened.

00:11:59.400 --> 00:12:03.060
And it took about 12 to 14 hours
for us to recover from that.

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:06.052
Luckily, the good
part of that was

00:12:06.052 --> 00:12:08.670
that none of the public-facing
node pools were down.

00:12:08.670 --> 00:12:11.330
But obviously from
enduring use case,

00:12:11.330 --> 00:12:13.980
for anyone to access
as GKE cluster,

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:17.400
we have to go via the control
frame, what we call it

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:22.260
as the Cube CTLD portal, that
is the terminal window where we

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:24.930
log in and look at our
node pools and resources,

00:12:24.930 --> 00:12:25.770
so on and so forth.

00:12:25.770 --> 00:12:28.300
We weren't able to access it.

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:30.900
But luckily, we had
very good support

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:34.350
from the Google product team
to help us resolve the issue.

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:36.240
Eventually, we found
out that there is a way

00:12:36.240 --> 00:12:39.090
to work with the
cluster management

00:12:39.090 --> 00:12:42.660
issue via this control
plane to like share

00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:43.830
the loads between the zones.

00:12:43.830 --> 00:12:46.980
And then we started getting
into that kind of conversations

00:12:46.980 --> 00:12:49.500
with the Google platform
support specialists.

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:52.740
And it was just, while it
was already challenging,

00:12:52.740 --> 00:12:56.070
because obviously train doors,
US is operating with panic

00:12:56.070 --> 00:12:58.680
saying, that, hey, I'm not able
to look at what is happening

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:00.090
in my own production.

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:02.580
But eventually, it's
a good learning curve,

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:04.530
because that's where
we also got lots

00:13:04.530 --> 00:13:08.152
of these important things, what
we do on the cloud platform.

00:13:08.152 --> 00:13:10.110
ROMIN IRANI: That was an
interesting challenge,

00:13:10.110 --> 00:13:14.160
and thanks, Ram, for taking
us into how you addressed it.

00:13:14.160 --> 00:13:17.850
What I wanted to do now is
actually go back to the vehicle

00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:18.570
itself.

00:13:18.570 --> 00:13:21.030
And earlier you
mentioned that there's

00:13:21.030 --> 00:13:23.310
a device that's running
on the vehicle itself,

00:13:23.310 --> 00:13:24.490
right at the edge.

00:13:24.490 --> 00:13:26.700
So if you could tell
us a little bit more

00:13:26.700 --> 00:13:30.580
about the features of the device
that is there on the vehicle.

00:13:30.580 --> 00:13:32.520
RAM BHAVARAJU: So
vehicle intelligence

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:35.620
as a platform, if we say
put that word across, right?

00:13:35.620 --> 00:13:39.330
So we are trying to make
a decision, the vehicle

00:13:39.330 --> 00:13:42.510
and the cloud almost
having like similar or more

00:13:42.510 --> 00:13:43.560
functionalities.

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:45.810
But there are limitations
in terms of vehicle,

00:13:45.810 --> 00:13:47.880
just because of the
hardware limitations,

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:51.340
and also the form factor, what
you can fit within the vehicle.

00:13:51.340 --> 00:13:53.370
And that's the very
fact that intelligence

00:13:53.370 --> 00:13:57.272
is like a little bit of what
is a compact in that sense.

00:13:57.272 --> 00:13:59.355
We have multiple layers
within the vehicle itself.

00:13:59.355 --> 00:14:02.580
So we have the dashboard layer,
which is the human machine

00:14:02.580 --> 00:14:04.300
interface or a chimney
what we call it,

00:14:04.300 --> 00:14:08.850
which has a UI app, which is
built on Android platform.

00:14:08.850 --> 00:14:11.670
Then we have applications
which are supporting this HMA,

00:14:11.670 --> 00:14:13.660
and also taking lots
of intelligent steps.

00:14:13.660 --> 00:14:18.130
So we have data intelligence
as a library or an application,

00:14:18.130 --> 00:14:23.220
which runs, which takes
informed decisions around,

00:14:23.220 --> 00:14:27.280
I'll give a feature like
auto indicator turn-off.

00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:30.090
So auto indicator turn-off
is something which

00:14:30.090 --> 00:14:32.010
you would have seen in a car.

00:14:32.010 --> 00:14:35.880
And automatically, as soon as
you take it on, it gets back,

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:37.530
the lever pulls back, right?

00:14:37.530 --> 00:14:40.440
A similar kind of a thing
in a tubular braking,

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:43.450
and probably you have not
heard or seen about it, right,

00:14:43.450 --> 00:14:46.060
so that's one thing as
an intelligent platform,

00:14:46.060 --> 00:14:46.980
you're able to build.

00:14:46.980 --> 00:14:50.580
Because of these sensors, what
their depth within the vehicle,

00:14:50.580 --> 00:14:54.060
we are able to get the data and
auto turn off the indicators.

00:14:54.060 --> 00:14:55.680
And these are some
of those features

00:14:55.680 --> 00:14:58.650
which the data intelligence
platform is building.

00:14:58.650 --> 00:15:02.280
And that's where that data
intelligence as service

00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:03.900
which is inside the vehicle.

00:15:03.900 --> 00:15:07.140
Then we have a layer called the
networking layer or the edge

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:10.140
device, which is right at
the top of all the layers

00:15:10.140 --> 00:15:11.310
within the vehicle, right?

00:15:11.310 --> 00:15:16.680
And this is the one which takes
care of your entire connection

00:15:16.680 --> 00:15:19.530
to the upstream, that is
your CloudConnect connecting

00:15:19.530 --> 00:15:22.320
to the cloud or what
we call it as a V2X,

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:25.270
vehicle to the
cloud in this case.

00:15:25.270 --> 00:15:27.250
This is an edge device
oriented application,

00:15:27.250 --> 00:15:29.100
which sits right on
the networking layer,

00:15:29.100 --> 00:15:31.380
and sends its data to
the Artemis group of what

00:15:31.380 --> 00:15:34.870
we spoke about during
the cloud side of things.

00:15:34.870 --> 00:15:37.620
It also takes care of one
very, very important or salient

00:15:37.620 --> 00:15:39.330
feature, and that's
the reason we call it

00:15:39.330 --> 00:15:42.110
as an intelligent or data
intelligence, in this case,

00:15:42.110 --> 00:15:44.330
right?

00:15:44.330 --> 00:15:46.685
Because we have a 4G
Sim inside the vehicle,

00:15:46.685 --> 00:15:49.310
and you are roaming around, and
there's a data connection which

00:15:49.310 --> 00:15:52.020
is being there, not there.

00:15:52.020 --> 00:15:55.520
And in the situations where the
data connection is not there,

00:15:55.520 --> 00:15:59.450
this edge application
starts storing the data

00:15:59.450 --> 00:16:00.680
within the vehicle.

00:16:00.680 --> 00:16:03.240
And now the challenge
is that how long can it

00:16:03.240 --> 00:16:06.560
store, because you're talking
about gigabytes, terabytes,

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:09.320
and petabytes on
the cloud, right?

00:16:09.320 --> 00:16:11.182
That's where the
intelligence part of things

00:16:11.182 --> 00:16:12.140
comes into the picture.

00:16:12.140 --> 00:16:14.960
It can store up to
21 days of data.

00:16:14.960 --> 00:16:17.270
Then we also have a very,
very important application

00:16:17.270 --> 00:16:20.540
called as a shadow
application, probably a vehicle

00:16:20.540 --> 00:16:23.150
and make an application
we call vehicular.

00:16:23.150 --> 00:16:26.120
This transforms
all the data, which

00:16:26.120 --> 00:16:29.870
is coming from the
internal units,

00:16:29.870 --> 00:16:33.350
or the control units within
this electric vehicle,

00:16:33.350 --> 00:16:36.410
and sends it to the edge
device or edge application,

00:16:36.410 --> 00:16:38.420
and sends its
outputs to the cloud.

00:16:38.420 --> 00:16:40.940
It kind of gets all
the data and publishes

00:16:40.940 --> 00:16:43.790
to this edge application,
which further publishes up

00:16:43.790 --> 00:16:45.110
into the cloud.

00:16:45.110 --> 00:16:48.828
Similarly, we also have
a very salient feature

00:16:48.828 --> 00:16:50.870
called this feature flag
management system, which

00:16:50.870 --> 00:16:53.900
is like a system
which can manage all

00:16:53.900 --> 00:16:56.600
the features
outside the vehicle,

00:16:56.600 --> 00:17:00.020
like for example, a
customer comes to us,

00:17:00.020 --> 00:17:03.230
says that I want to do a
different substitution plan,

00:17:03.230 --> 00:17:05.630
or change, add
more features, turn

00:17:05.630 --> 00:17:07.760
on more features inside
my vehicle, right?

00:17:07.760 --> 00:17:10.339
And that is available right
now at this point in time,

00:17:10.339 --> 00:17:13.520
where we will send that
back into the vehicle.

00:17:13.520 --> 00:17:14.900
And this vehicle
app is one which

00:17:14.900 --> 00:17:16.700
is kind of getting
those commands

00:17:16.700 --> 00:17:20.569
and transforming that into those
control units in the language

00:17:20.569 --> 00:17:22.819
they understand, or
probably in the command

00:17:22.819 --> 00:17:24.200
they would understand.

00:17:24.200 --> 00:17:26.579
And so all these layers
are stacked up that way.

00:17:26.579 --> 00:17:28.700
You have the edge device which
is sitting right on the edge.

00:17:28.700 --> 00:17:31.200
Then you have the dashboard,
which is sitting on the vehicle

00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:34.187
and informing the customer,
take some informed decisions.

00:17:34.187 --> 00:17:36.020
Then you have this
entire data intelligence,

00:17:36.020 --> 00:17:37.730
which is kind of
reading the data,

00:17:37.730 --> 00:17:40.850
and making more informed
decisions within the vehicle

00:17:40.850 --> 00:17:42.440
and also sending it upwards.

00:17:42.440 --> 00:17:44.370
Then the in-vehicle
aboard the shadow,

00:17:44.370 --> 00:17:46.760
which is talking
both ways, that is,

00:17:46.760 --> 00:17:50.420
to the cloud and from the
cloud, and sending further down

00:17:50.420 --> 00:17:54.038
into the different
units within the--

00:17:54.038 --> 00:17:55.580
ROMIN IRANI: Thanks,
Ram, for helping

00:17:55.580 --> 00:17:58.280
us understand the kind of
intelligence that's built

00:17:58.280 --> 00:18:01.700
in every Ather Energy vehicle.

00:18:01.700 --> 00:18:05.120
Now, if I just look
at the whole platform

00:18:05.120 --> 00:18:08.510
and where you are there right
now in terms of the features,

00:18:08.510 --> 00:18:11.660
when you started off, right,
to build out this platform,

00:18:11.660 --> 00:18:14.600
there must be certain, I
would say, characteristics,

00:18:14.600 --> 00:18:17.090
or certain kind of
features, or the way

00:18:17.090 --> 00:18:20.450
you would want this platform to
have turned out in the future.

00:18:20.450 --> 00:18:24.050
And what were some of
those technology decisions

00:18:24.050 --> 00:18:27.080
that you had to make
at that point in time,

00:18:27.080 --> 00:18:29.540
even in terms of the vision
you had for the product?

00:18:29.540 --> 00:18:31.220
And how has Google
Cloud platform

00:18:31.220 --> 00:18:32.390
helped you achieve that?

00:18:32.390 --> 00:18:34.265
RAM BHAVARAJU: Obviously,
the top of the list

00:18:34.265 --> 00:18:36.830
is like cutting down
the cost as you scale.

00:18:36.830 --> 00:18:40.640
So when you're at 1,000-2,000
vehicles, like a startup,

00:18:40.640 --> 00:18:43.560
you would always think about
how do I go fast out there.

00:18:43.560 --> 00:18:46.280
But once you start moving
from 2,000, like 10,000,

00:18:46.280 --> 00:18:48.740
20,000, and upwards, right?

00:18:48.740 --> 00:18:50.195
You have to cut down the cost.

00:18:50.195 --> 00:18:53.010
Cost was one of our
major drag things.

00:18:53.010 --> 00:18:54.860
And that's where GCP
was very, very helpful.

00:18:54.860 --> 00:18:57.550
In fact, the entire
case of GKE is

00:18:57.550 --> 00:19:00.660
quite good in terms
of optimize workloads,

00:19:00.660 --> 00:19:05.425
like auto-scaling, the
auto-scaling features,

00:19:05.425 --> 00:19:07.550
to increase their workloads,
reduce their workloads

00:19:07.550 --> 00:19:09.260
on demand.

00:19:09.260 --> 00:19:12.200
And something like preemptible
virtual machines or PBMs,

00:19:12.200 --> 00:19:13.760
what you call them
as, to be attached

00:19:13.760 --> 00:19:16.370
to these workloads, which
inherently saves lots

00:19:16.370 --> 00:19:18.080
of cost in that sense, right?

00:19:18.080 --> 00:19:21.650
So we were able to balance all
these tools, all these managed

00:19:21.650 --> 00:19:22.950
services together.

00:19:22.950 --> 00:19:26.000
And one of the key things
about this informed decision

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:29.030
of being data on
Google Cloud Platform

00:19:29.030 --> 00:19:32.990
is the kind of awareness around
the open source platform,

00:19:32.990 --> 00:19:35.630
and the technologies,
what GCP brings in.

00:19:35.630 --> 00:19:39.660
So and we have a blend of very
good open source technologies.

00:19:39.660 --> 00:19:41.840
And we also have
enterprise level support

00:19:41.840 --> 00:19:43.040
for those technologies.

00:19:43.040 --> 00:19:45.180
And that's a good
blend of things, right?

00:19:45.180 --> 00:19:49.070
So that's very inherently,
GCP understands

00:19:49.070 --> 00:19:52.070
the needs of the customers
to a large extent.

00:19:52.070 --> 00:19:56.120
And that's one of the key
things for any company

00:19:56.120 --> 00:19:59.767
to take this decision, not
just data, because you'll

00:19:59.767 --> 00:20:01.850
have also something called
as a community support.

00:20:01.850 --> 00:20:05.690
So you have this expert support
in terms of how to run systems,

00:20:05.690 --> 00:20:08.150
and then you have this
community support, too,

00:20:08.150 --> 00:20:09.617
how to build systems.

00:20:09.617 --> 00:20:11.450
So you need both the
worlds together, right?

00:20:11.450 --> 00:20:14.870
So until both the worlds
are like seamlessly working

00:20:14.870 --> 00:20:17.540
together, it's very,
very tough to have

00:20:17.540 --> 00:20:22.760
that kind of like, what do you
say, a marriage in this case,

00:20:22.760 --> 00:20:26.690
right? to run the entire
production systems out there.

00:20:26.690 --> 00:20:28.760
And that's it, GCP was
very, very, very helpful.

00:20:28.760 --> 00:20:30.580
And that kind of
maturity in terms

00:20:30.580 --> 00:20:32.330
of understanding the
open-source platforms

00:20:32.330 --> 00:20:35.790
and getting them into the
mainstream of managed services.

00:20:35.790 --> 00:20:39.480
So I'll give a very, very
clear example, in this case.

00:20:39.480 --> 00:20:41.960
We were looking
at hive metastore,

00:20:41.960 --> 00:20:44.420
to be available as a
managed service within GCP.

00:20:44.420 --> 00:20:47.510
And luckily, or
probably, Google also

00:20:47.510 --> 00:20:49.080
was thinking about
the same thing.

00:20:49.080 --> 00:20:51.410
And GCP was really saying,
hey, hive metastore

00:20:51.410 --> 00:20:53.300
has now generic availability.

00:20:53.300 --> 00:20:56.600
You don't have to go and build
it like a workload within GKE.

00:20:56.600 --> 00:21:00.140
We have it as a G, or
generic availability.

00:21:00.140 --> 00:21:03.620
It means that we can turn on
the service whenever we want it.

00:21:03.620 --> 00:21:05.810
So that gives a
very good example

00:21:05.810 --> 00:21:10.610
of how Google helps and listen
to the customers in this case,

00:21:10.610 --> 00:21:12.822
and being very
proactive in terms

00:21:12.822 --> 00:21:14.780
of understanding those
open source technologies

00:21:14.780 --> 00:21:18.470
and bringing them into a
good product treatment.

